+.TH Tk_UpdatePointer 3 8.1 Tk "Tk Library Procedures"
+.so man.macros
+.BS
+.SH NAME
+Tk_UpdatePointer \- feed native pointer state into Tk's pointer emulation
+.SH SYNOPSIS
+.nf
+\fB#include <tk.h>\fR
+.sp
+\fBTk_UpdatePointer\fR(\fItkwin, x, y, state\fR)
+.fi
+.SH ARGUMENTS
+.AS Tk_Window tkwin
+.AP Tk_Window tkwin in
+Window that currently contains the pointer, or NULL if the pointer is
+not over any window managed by Tk.
+.AP int x in
+X coordinate of the pointer, in root window coordinates.
+.AP int y in
+Y coordinate of the pointer, in root window coordinates.
+.AP int state in
+Current state of the mouse buttons and keyboard modifiers, as a mask of
+X11 state bits such as \fBButton1Mask\fR, \fBShiftMask\fR or
+\fBControlMask\fR.
+.BE
+.SH DESCRIPTION
+.PP
+On platforms where Tk is not running on top of an X server (Windows
+and macOS), Tk emulates the X11 pointer model in software.
+\fBTk_UpdatePointer\fR is the entry point to that emulation: it is
+called with the current pointer window, position and button/modifier
+state, compares them with the state from the previous call, and queues
+the corresponding \fBEnterNotify\fR, \fBLeaveNotify\fR,
+\fBMotionNotify\fR, \fBButtonPress\fR and \fBButtonRelease\fR events
+with \fBTk_QueueWindowEvent\fR. It also maintains the implicit button
+grab and honors a global grab set with \fBgrab\fR.
+.PP
+Tk calls this function itself from its native event handlers; an
+extension only needs it if it receives native mouse events that Tk does
+not see (for example in an embedded foreign window) and wants Tk
+widgets to behave as if the pointer had moved or a button had changed
+state.
+.PP
+On X11 the X server provides these events directly, and
+\fBTk_UpdatePointer\fR does nothing.
+.SH "SEE ALSO"
+Tk_QueueWindowEvent(3), grab(n)
+.SH KEYWORDS
+pointer, mouse, button, enter, leave, motion, grab, event
